<input kind="directory" status="ok" bytes="1256">
<file path="research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md">
# Demo Positioning Notes

- A research agent is a useful base demo because the input and output are familiar: ask a question, wait for work, receive a report.
- Subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for the final synthesis.
- Working memory makes the run tangible because users can inspect generated scout reports and final reports on disk inside the run's research session directory.
- The trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.

</file>

<file path="research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md">
# Kernel Architecture Notes

- The kernel owns generic runtime concerns: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading.
- The host application owns product semantics: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.
- Filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`.
- Context loaders are declared by each agent and resolved through a catalog that combines portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.

</file>
</input>

</synthesis_writer_context>"
,
                            "raw": "{"start":{"eventId":"4b27b861-2649-46b3-a98a-880834b7a05a","containerId":"8e60b276-ada2-5668-9a14-67003435e0fe","runId":"fd983bca-fc23-4190-8cab-475555c3304e","piSessionId":"019f24bc-aadb-77fd-84f6-1930fb495665","agentId":null,"userId":null,"type":"context_build_started","source":"kernel","traceLevel":1,"eventData":{"agent_name":"report-writer","declared_inputs":[{"kind":"file","ref":"research-memory/brief.md"},{"kind":"working-memory","ref":"research-memory/scout-reports"},{"kind":"directory","ref":"research-memory/sources/**/*.md"}]},"spanId":"7626a000-8aaf-45a2-a9ae-19a953cde460","parentEventId":"a08803c8-df7f-48cb-875f-552dcf825dc7","timestamp":"2026-07-02T21:29:32.132Z"},"end":{"eventId":"41c16c9a-4e9f-4e67-974c-904e1de4a445","containerId":"8e60b276-ada2-5668-9a14-67003435e0fe","runId":"fd983bca-fc23-4190-8cab-475555c3304e","piSessionId":"019f24bc-aadb-77fd-84f6-1930fb495665","agentId":null,"userId":null,"type":"context_build_completed","source":"kernel","traceLevel":1,"eventData":{"inputs":[{"loader_kind":"file","input_ref":"research-memory/brief.md","status":"ok","bytes":1292},{"loader_kind":"working-memory","input_ref":"research-memory/scout-reports","status":"ok","bytes":13005},{"loader_kind":"directory","input_ref":"research-memory/sources/**/*.md","status":"ok","bytes":1256}],"rendered_context":"<synthesis_writer_context>\\n\\n<focus>Write the final report answering: Research the next step for turning this simple kernel into a richer agent experience. Use the brief, source notes, and both scout reports. Recommend the next concrete increment, explain why it is the right base-demo step, preserve the kernel/app boundary, and note residual unknowns. The report should stand alone for the operator.</focus>\\n\\n<input kind=\\"file\\" status=\\"ok\\" bytes=\\"1292\\">\\n# Simple Research Kernel Brief\\n\\nThis example application demonstrates the Agent Kernel as a runnable Simple Research Kernel.\\n\\nThe intended flow is:\\n\\n1. A user submits a research request.\\n2. The app creates one research session directory for the run and copies this seed brief/source material into that session's \`research-memory/\` folder.\\n3. A coordinator reads the brief and working memory from the active research session.\\n4. The coordinator dispatches focused scout subagents.\\n5. The coordinator waits for all scouts to return their reports.\\n6. The coordinator reads the scout reports and spawns follow-up scouts if coverage is incomplete.\\n7. The coordinator queues a report writer.\\n8. The report writer reads accumulated memory and returns a final report.\\n\\nThe demo is deliberately local in its source material, but the run itself uses a live model/tool loop. A valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for their markdown reports under the run's \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report under the run's \`research-memory/reports/\`. If credentials or runtime services are missing, the harness should fail honestly rather than fabricating a trace.\\n\\n</input>\\n\\n<input kind=\\"working-memory\\" status=\\"ok\\" bytes=\\"13005\\">\\n<working_memory path=\\"research-memory/scout-reports/01-kernel-runtime-increment-rich-agent-experience.md\\">\\n# Runtime Agent Orchestration and Trace Increment\\n\\nPrompt: Original request: Research the next step for turning this simple kernel into a richer agent experience. Narrow focus: From the loaded brief and source notes, identify the most important kernel/runtime architecture increment that would make this simple research kernel feel like a richer agent experience while preserving the kernel/app boundary.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Investigated the loaded brief and source notes for architecture responsibilities and the next runtime increment that would most improve perceived agent richness.\\n- Focused on kernel/app boundary, context loading, subagent management, protocol events, trace reading, and filesystem agent definitions.\\n- Intentionally left UI design, model/provider selection, external web research, and domain-specific research tool design for other scouts or final synthesis.\\n\\n## Observations\\n- The simple research kernel is already framed as a multi-agent workflow: user request, session setup, coordinator context read, scout dispatch, scout report collection, follow-up scouts, report-writer queueing, and final report writing.\\n- The richest missing increment appears to be a kernel-owned orchestration/observability runtime that makes this workflow explicit as protocol events and readable traces, rather than leaving richness only as files appearing on disk.\\n- Kernel-owned behavior should remain generic:\\n  - \`createKernel\`\\n  - run context\\n  - agent registry\\n  - context assembly\\n  - subagent management\\n  - protocol events\\n  - trace reading\\n- App-owned behavior should remain product/domain-specific:\\n  - agent catalog\\n  - domain tools\\n  - app-specific loaders\\n  - session mapping\\n  - generated artifacts such as scout reports and final reports in \`research-memory/\`\\n- Context loading is the seam that preserves this boundary. Agents declare context loaders; a catalog resolves them by combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n- Filesystem agent definitions are a practical next-step anchor: \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\` can define richer agents without hard-coding research semantics into the kernel.\\n- The brief’s expected valid run strongly suggests the increment should support visible nested work: coordinator reads session-scoped context, spawns source-scout subagents, waits for reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviews them, queues the report writer, and writes a final report under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- The demo-positioning notes emphasize that users should be able to inspect both tangible working memory on disk and an end-to-end trace: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` defines the intended run flow: user submits request; app creates a research session directory and copies seed material into \`research-memory/\`; coordinator reads brief and working memory; coordinator dispatches focused scout subagents; coordinator waits for reports; coordinator may spawn follow-up scouts; coordinator queues report writer; final report is written.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` says a valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for markdown reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that the kernel owns generic runtime concerns: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that the host application owns product semantics: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that context loaders are declared by each agent and resolved through a catalog combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for final synthesis.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says working memory makes the run tangible because generated scout reports and final reports are inspectable on disk inside the run’s research session directory.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says the trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n- Prioritize a kernel-level orchestration and observability increment: implement/solidify generic subagent lifecycle management, protocol event emission, and trace reading around filesystem-defined agents and declared context loaders. Keep research-specific semantics in the app: \`src/agent-catalog\`, session-to-\`research-memory\` mapping, source-scout/report-writer definitions, and artifacts under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\` and \`research-memory/reports/\`. This would make the demo feel like a richer agent experience because users could see nested agents, context assembly, tool calls, waits, and outputs as an inspectable trace while still preserving the kernel/app boundary.\\n\\n## Residual Questions\\n- The loaded context does not show actual source tree implementation details such as exact \`src/agent-catalog\` files, \`/kernel/*\` module names, or current trace event schema. The final writer should avoid claiming specific implementation status beyond the loaded notes unless additional source evidence is available.\\n\\n</working_memory>\\n\\n<working_memory path=\\"research-memory/scout-reports/02-product-demo-increment-inspectable-traced-research-run.md\\">\\n# Make the Research Run Inspectable and Traceable\\n\\nPrompt: Original request: Research the next step for turning this simple kernel into a richer agent experience. Narrow focus: From the loaded brief and source notes, identify the most important product/demo increment that would make the simple research kernel more compelling to users and viewers.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Investigated the loaded brief and source notes for the product/demo increment most likely to make the Simple Research Kernel feel richer and more compelling.\\n- Focused on evidence about research-agent familiarity, focused scout subagents, inspectable working memory, session-scoped artifacts, trace viewer story, and honest failure behavior.\\n- Intentionally left implementation-level details of UI design, trace schema, model selection, and external/web research integrations for other scouts or later planning.\\n\\n## Observations\\n- The strongest product/demo increment is not adding broad new research capability; it is making the existing multi-agent run visibly inspectable end-to-end: a session-scoped research workspace plus trace viewer that shows coordinator behavior, scout subagents, tool/context activity, generated markdown artifacts, and final synthesis.\\n- The research-agent pattern is already positioned as familiar to users: users ask a question, wait while agents work, and receive a report. This means the next increment should emphasize legibility and trust rather than explain a novel workflow from scratch.\\n- Focused scout subagents are a core demo affordance. They make delegation understandable because each scout can be framed as gathering a narrow report for the coordinator/report writer instead of acting as an opaque general agent.\\n- Inspectable working memory is the most concrete differentiator in the loaded context. Generated scout reports and final reports on disk make the run tangible and auditable.\\n- Session-scoped artifacts are central to the intended flow. The brief says each run should create a research session directory, copy seed material into \`research-memory/\`, write scout reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, and write final reports under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- The trace viewer story should complement the filesystem artifacts: it can show prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output. Together, traces explain process while working-memory files preserve outputs.\\n- Kernel-owned behavior should remain generic: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading. The richer experience should avoid baking research-specific semantics into the kernel.\\n- App-owned behavior should provide the compelling product shape: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts. The simple research app should own the research-session directory and artifact conventions.\\n- Failure honesty is part of the demo value. The brief explicitly says the harness should fail honestly when credentials or runtime services are missing rather than fabricating a trace. A compelling viewer/demo should preserve this by showing real runs and clear missing-prerequisite failures.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` describes the intended flow: user submits a request; the app creates one research session directory; the coordinator reads the brief and working memory; the coordinator dispatches focused scout subagents; scouts write markdown reports; the coordinator may spawn follow-up scouts; then a report writer writes the final report.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` specifies concrete session-scoped artifact locations: seed material copied into \`research-memory/\`, scout reports under the run's \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, and final reports under the run's \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` says a valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for markdown reports, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` also establishes failure honesty: if credentials or runtime services are missing, the harness should fail honestly rather than fabricating a trace.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says a research agent is a useful base demo because the input/output pattern is familiar: ask a question, wait for work, receive a report.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for final synthesis.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says working memory makes the run tangible because users can inspect generated scout reports and final reports on disk inside the run's research session directory.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says the trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` distinguishes kernel-owned generic runtime concerns from host application-owned product semantics. Kernel-owned examples include \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading. Host-owned examples include agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` notes that filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`, and that context loaders are resolved through a catalog combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders. This supports a demo where the app remains understandable and inspectable without hard-coding product semantics into the kernel.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n- Prioritize an “inspectable research run” product/demo increment: after a user launches a research request, show a session page or CLI summary that links together the live trace and the run directory artifacts. The demo should explicitly surface: the active research-session path, loaded context, coordinator step, each focused scout subagent, each generated \`research-memory/scout-reports/*.md\` file, the final \`research-memory/reports/*.md\` report, and any honest runtime failure state. This would make the existing kernel capabilities feel richer without expanding scope into unrelated agent features.\\n\\n## Residual Questions\\n- The loaded context does not specify the current state of any UI or trace viewer implementation, so the final report should avoid claiming that a viewer already exists unless other evidence confirms it.\\n- The loaded context does not define exact trace event names or artifact naming conventions beyond the directory-level paths, so implementation recommendations should stay at the product/story level unless supplemented by code inspection.\\n\\n</working_memory>\\n</input>\\n\\n<input kind=\\"directory\\" status=\\"ok\\" bytes=\\"1256\\">\\n<file path=\\"research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\\">\\n# Demo Positioning Notes\\n\\n- A research agent is a useful base demo because the input and output are familiar: ask a question, wait for work, receive a report.\\n- Subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for the final synthesis.\\n- Working memory makes the run tangible because users can inspect generated scout reports and final reports on disk inside the run's research session directory.\\n- The trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\n</file>\\n\\n<file path=\\"research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\\">\\n# Kernel Architecture Notes\\n\\n- The kernel owns generic runtime concerns: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading.\\n- The host application owns product semantics: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n- Filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`.\\n- Context loaders are declared by each agent and resolved through a catalog that combines portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n\\n</file>\\n</input>\\n\\n</synthesis_writer_context>","total_bytes":16174},"sp