tely local in its source material, but the run itself uses a live model/tool loop. A valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for their markdown reports under the run's \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report under the run's \`research-memory/reports/\`. If credentials or runtime services are missing, the harness should fail honestly rather than fabricating a trace.\\n\\n</file>\\n\\n<file path=\\"research-memory/scout-reports/01-kernel-runtime-increment-rich-agent-experience.md\\" bytes=\\"5815\\" truncated=\\"false\\">\\n# Runtime Agent Orchestration and Trace Increment\\n\\nPrompt: Original request: Research the next step for turning this simple kernel into a richer agent experience. Narrow focus: From the loaded brief and source notes, identify the most important kernel/runtime architecture increment that would make this simple research kernel feel like a richer agent experience while preserving the kernel/app boundary.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Investigated the loaded brief and source notes for architecture responsibilities and the next runtime increment that would most improve perceived agent richness.\\n- Focused on kernel/app boundary, context loading, subagent management, protocol events, trace reading, and filesystem agent definitions.\\n- Intentionally left UI design, model/provider selection, external web research, and domain-specific research tool design for other scouts or final synthesis.\\n\\n## Observations\\n- The simple research kernel is already framed as a multi-agent workflow: user request, session setup, coordinator context read, scout dispatch, scout report collection, follow-up scouts, report-writer queueing, and final report writing.\\n- The richest missing increment appears to be a kernel-owned orchestration/observability runtime that makes this workflow explicit as protocol events and readable traces, rather than leaving richness only as files appearing on disk.\\n- Kernel-owned behavior should remain generic:\\n  - \`createKernel\`\\n  - run context\\n  - agent registry\\n  - context assembly\\n  - subagent management\\n  - protocol events\\n  - trace reading\\n- App-owned behavior should remain product/domain-specific:\\n  - agent catalog\\n  - domain tools\\n  - app-specific loaders\\n  - session mapping\\n  - generated artifacts such as scout reports and final reports in \`research-memory/\`\\n- Context loading is the seam that preserves this boundary. Agents declare context loaders; a catalog resolves them by combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n- Filesystem agent definitions are a practical next-step anchor: \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\` can define richer agents without hard-coding research semantics into the kernel.\\n- The brief’s expected valid run strongly suggests the increment should support visible nested work: coordinator reads session-scoped context, spawns source-scout subagents, waits for reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviews them, queues the report writer, and writes a final report under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- The demo-positioning notes emphasize that users should be able to inspect both tangible working memory on disk and an end-to-end trace: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` defines the intended run flow: user submits request; app creates a research session directory and copies seed material into \`research-memory/\`; coordinator reads brief and working memory; coordinator dispatches focused scout subagents; coordinator waits for reports; coordinator may spawn follow-up scouts; coordinator queues report writer; final report is written.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` says a valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for markdown reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that the kernel owns generic runtime concerns: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that the host application owns product semantics: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` states that context loaders are declared by each agent and resolved through a catalog combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for final synthesis.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says working memory makes the run tangible because generated scout reports and final reports are inspectable on disk inside the run’s research session directory.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says the trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n- Prioritize a kernel-level orchestration and observability increment: implement/solidify generic subagent lifecycle management, protocol event emission, and trace reading around filesystem-defined agents and declared context loaders. Keep research-specific semantics in the app: \`src/agent-catalog\`, session-to-\`research-memory\` mapping, source-scout/report-writer definitions, and artifacts under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\` and \`research-memory/reports/\`. This would make the demo feel like a richer agent experience because users could see nested agents, context assembly, tool calls, waits, and outputs as an inspectable trace while still preserving the kernel/app boundary.\\n\\n## Residual Questions\\n- The loaded context does not show actual source tree implementation details such as exact \`src/agent-catalog\` files, \`/kernel/*\` module names, or current trace event schema. The final writer should avoid claiming specific implementation status beyond the loaded notes unless additional source evidence is available.\\n\\n</file>\\n\\n<file path=\\"research-memory/scout-reports/02-product-demo-increment-inspectable-traced-research-run.md\\" bytes=\\"6930\\" truncated=\\"false\\">\\n# Make the Research Run Inspectable and Traceable\\n\\nPrompt: Original request: Research the next step for turning this simple kernel into a richer agent experience. Narrow focus: From the loaded brief and source notes, identify the most important product/demo increment that would make the simple research kernel more compelling to users and viewers.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Investigated the loaded brief and source notes for the product/demo increment most likely to make the Simple Research Kernel feel richer and more compelling.\\n- Focused on evidence about research-agent familiarity, focused scout subagents, inspectable working memory, session-scoped artifacts, trace viewer story, and honest failure behavior.\\n- Intentionally left implementation-level details of UI design, trace schema, model selection, and external/web research integrations for other scouts or later planning.\\n\\n## Observations\\n- The strongest product/demo increment is not adding broad new research capability; it is making the existing multi-agent run visibly inspectable end-to-end: a session-scoped research workspace plus trace viewer that shows coordinator behavior, scout subagents, tool/context activity, generated markdown artifacts, and final synthesis.\\n- The research-agent pattern is already positioned as familiar to users: users ask a question, wait while agents work, and receive a report. This means the next increment should emphasize legibility and trust rather than explain a novel workflow from scratch.\\n- Focused scout subagents are a core demo affordance. They make delegation understandable because each scout can be framed as gathering a narrow report for the coordinator/report writer instead of acting as an opaque general agent.\\n- Inspectable working memory is the most concrete differentiator in the loaded context. Generated scout reports and final reports on disk make the run tangible and auditable.\\n- Session-scoped artifacts are central to the intended flow. The brief says each run should create a research session directory, copy seed material into \`research-memory/\`, write scout reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, and write final reports under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- The trace viewer story should complement the filesystem artifacts: it can show prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output. Together, traces explain process while working-memory files preserve outputs.\\n- Kernel-owned behavior should remain generic: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assemb"},"spanId":"call_U3sweS8cpC5UT9yLVE4dwBQe|fc_0eed65e0aac72b64016a46d83d93d48198885560b91ed4c23b","parentEventId":null,"timestamp":"2026-07-02T21:29:33.701Z"}}",
