les\\n\\n- **Coordinator**\\n  - Reads the session-scoped brief and working memory.\\n  - Decides which focused scouts are needed.\\n  - Dispatches scout subagents.\\n  - Waits for scout reports under \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`.\\n  - Reviews coverage and can spawn follow-up scouts if needed.\\n  - Queues the report writer once enough evidence exists.\\n\\n- **Source scout**\\n  - Performs a narrow investigation against the loaded local source notes and brief.\\n  - Produces a markdown scout report in \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`.\\n  - In this run, one scout focused on the kernel/runtime architecture increment and one focused on the product/demo increment.\\n\\n- **Report writer**\\n  - Reads the accumulated brief, source notes, scout reports, and session memory layout.\\n  - Synthesizes the final operator-facing answer.\\n  - Writes the final report under \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n\\n## Session Working Memory Layout\\n\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\`\\n  - The run brief and expected workflow.\\n\\n- \`research-memory/sources/\`\\n  - Local source notes used as the research corpus.\\n  - In this run, this included architecture and demo-positioning notes.\\n\\n- \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`\\n  - Intermediate markdown reports produced by focused scout subagents.\\n\\n- \`research-memory/reports/\`\\n  - Final markdown reports produced by the report writer.\\n\\n## Evidence From This Run\\n\\nScout reports used:\\n\\n- \`research-memory/scout-reports/01-kernel-runtime-increment-rich-agent-experience.md\`\\n  - Recommended a kernel-level orchestration and observability increment.\\n  - Emphasized generic subagent lifecycle management, protocol event emission, trace reading, filesystem-defined agents, and declared context loaders.\\n  - Warned not to claim specific implementation details beyond the loaded notes.\\n\\n- \`research-memory/scout-reports/02-product-demo-increment-inspectable-traced-research-run.md\`\\n  - Recommended an inspectable research-run demo increment.\\n  - Emphasized a session page or CLI summary linking live trace information to run-directory artifacts.\\n  - Called out honest runtime failure behavior as part of the demo value.\\n\\nSource notes used:\\n\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\`\\n  - Kernel owns generic runtime concerns: \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading.\\n  - Host app owns product semantics: agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n  - Filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`.\\n  - Context loaders are declared by agents and resolved through a catalog combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders.\\n\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\`\\n  - A research agent is a useful base demo because the input/output pattern is familiar.\\n  - Subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts.\\n  - Working memory makes the run tangible because reports are inspectable on disk.\\n  - A trace viewer can show prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n\\nBrief used:\\n\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\`\\n  - Defines the expected flow from user request through coordinator, scouts, report writer, and final report.\\n  - Specifies session-scoped files under \`research-memory/\`.\\n  - Requires the harness to fail honestly if credentials or runtime services are missing rather than fabricating a trace.\\n\\n## Why This Is A Good Base Demo\\n\\nResearch is the right base demo because it is easy for operators to understand without learning a new product domain. The user asks a question, waits while work happens, and receives a report. That makes it a strong vehicle for showing agent-kernel concepts.\\n\\nThe harness also demonstrates the core architecture cleanly:\\n\\n- The **workflow is multi-agent**,