directory; the coordinator reads the brief and working memory; the coordinator dispatches focused scout subagents; scouts write markdown reports; the coordinator may spawn follow-up scouts; then a report writer writes the final report.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` specifies concrete session-scoped artifact locations: seed material copied into \`research-memory/\`, scout reports under the run's \`research-memory/scout-reports/\`, and final reports under the run's \`research-memory/reports/\`.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` says a valid run should show the coordinator reading session-scoped context, spawning source-scout subagents, waiting for markdown reports, reviewing those reports, queueing the report writer, and writing a final report.\\n- \`research-memory/brief.md\` also establishes failure honesty: if credentials or runtime services are missing, the harness should fail honestly rather than fabricating a trace.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says a research agent is a useful base demo because the input/output pattern is familiar: ask a question, wait for work, receive a report.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says subagents are easy to explain as focused scouts that gather reports for final synthesis.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says working memory makes the run tangible because users can inspect generated scout reports and final reports on disk inside the run's research session directory.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/demo-positioning.md\` says the trace viewer can show the whole story: prompt resolution, context loading, tool calls, nested subagents, and final assistant output.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` distinguishes kernel-owned generic runtime concerns from host application-owned product semantics. Kernel-owned examples include \`createKernel\`, run context, agent registry, context assembly, subagent management, protocol events, and trace reading. Host-owned examples include agent catalog, domain tools, app-specific loaders, session mapping, and generated artifacts.\\n- \`research-memory/sources/kernel-architecture.md\` notes that filesystem agent definitions use \`agent.md\` plus optional sidecars such as \`context.ts\`, and that context loaders are resolved through a catalog combining portable kernel loaders with app-specific loaders. This supports a demo where the app remains understandable and inspectable without hard-coding product semantics into the kernel.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n- Prioritize an “inspectable research run” product/demo increment: after a user launches a research request, show a session page or CLI summary that links together the live trace and the run directory artifacts. The demo should explicitly surface: the active research-session path, loaded context, coordinator step, each focused scout subagent, each generated \`research-memory/scout-reports/*.md\` file, the final \`research-memory/reports/*.md\` report, and any honest runtime failure state. This would make the existing kernel capabilities feel richer without expanding scope into unrelated agent features.\\n\\n## Residual Questions\\n- The loaded context does not specify the current state of any UI or trace viewer implementation, so the final report should avoid claiming that a viewer already exists unless other evidence confirms it.\\n- The loaded context does not define exact trace event names or artifact naming conventions beyond the directory-level paths, so implementation recommendations should stay at the product/story level unless supplemented by code inspection."}}},"spanId":"call_wmpJoMGig5A8RBfbPMmIRBv1|fc_0197513652380de4016a46d81c31d0819bb3fdd9f0ef81bc1e","parentEventId":null,"timestamp":"2026-07-02T21:29:23.941Z"},"end":{"eventId":"b490a70f-615d-5d91-8c1e-023698af29ee","containerId":"8e60b276-ada2-5668-9a14-67003435e0fe","runId":"75205a44-0005-4a08-bdae-ca1afe6e4bf3","piSessionId":"019f24bc-1e52-71f7-8bf9-63c9d465cb4b","agentI